  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The state of Utah received $5.8B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Obligations are up 10.9% year over year. The federal buying footprint in Utah is concentrated: the Department of Defense obligated $4.2B, more than three-quarters of the statewide total. Federal awards in Utah are numerous but modest in size on average: there were 9,072 awards and the average award size was $639K. Year-to-date totals are partial for the fiscal year. Past annual totals show volatility: FY21 obligations were $4B, FY22 obligations were $5.3B, FY23 obligations were $6.3B, FY24 obligations were $7.2B, and FY25 obligations were $5.2B. Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing is the largest industry by obligations in FY2026 to date at $2.9B. Federal spending in Utah also channels significant dollars to Nonferrous Metal Rolling, Drawing, and Extruding at $563M and to Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $294M.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies drive federal contracting in Utah?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $4.2B in FY2026 to date. The Department of the Treasury is the second-largest buyer at $625M, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs at $320M and the Department of Agriculture at $146M. The General Services Administration, Department of Homeland Security, Department of the Interior, Department of Transportation, Department of Energy, and Department of Labor account for the remaining top-ten buying agencies with obligations ranging from $83.6M to $41.1M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors capture the most federal dollars in Utah?",
        "body": "Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing is the top industry with $2.9B in obligations. Nonferrous Metal (except Copper and Aluminum) Rolling, Drawing, and Extruding has $563M and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction has $294M. NORTHROP GRUMMAN SYSTEMS CORP is the largest vendor at $2.9B, with ASAHI REFINING USA INC at $563M and L3 TECHNOLOGIES, INC. at $290M also among the top recipients."
      }
    ]
  },
